BRAVE NEW WORLD ADVANCES LIKELY TO TRANSFORM SOCIETY

By Peggy Landers
Knight-Ridder


	Consider this: If you could design a genetically superior child,
free of disease and smart as a whip, would you?  Should you?

	Given that power, would conceiving a child in any other way be
socially irresponsible?  Or is manipulating the genetic code trespassing
in God's most holy domain?

	Which is more humane?  Caring for the diseased, the disabled and
the dysfunctional?  Or not bringing them into the world?

	Weighty questions, but not pie-in-the-sky.  The U.S. government's
Human Genome Project, a 15-year, $3 billion effort to map the 50,000 to
100,000 genes that code human life, makes such queries unnervingly
relevant.  The Genome Project, to be completed by 2005, promises to
revolutionize the way we define ourselves, our God and our way of life.

	Brave New World advances in molecular genetics will force American
society to examine its conscience in ways deeper and more profound than
ever before.  In just a couple of decades, scientists theorize, they will
be able to tell, almost from the moment of conception, whether a person is
susceptible to nearly 1,000 genetic disorders, including heart disease,
cancer, diabetes and mental illness.  Within 50 years they might be able
to change chromosomes and design a "boutique baby," a made-to-order human
with a stacked genetic deck.

	But who would do the designing?  Who would define "normal"?  Which
physical and behavioral traits would be chosen as "better"?  And why?
At what point would susceptibility to a disease warrant abortion?  And
what would a society devoid of diversity be like?

[Devoid of diversity?  The diversity of "heart disease, cancer, diabetes
 and mental illness"?!?  Sorry, I couldn't help editorializing.  -MV]

	"It could be that unless you are productive in the crassest
economic way, you're not valued," warns Tom Shannon, a religion and social
ethics professor at Worcester Polytechnic Institute in Massachusetts.

	Arthur Caplan, director of the Center for Biomedical Ethics at
the University of Minnesota, predicts: "By the time the 21st century comes
around, we'll wonder why we should put up with God's way of reproducing
ourselves.  We will perhaps decide that the way God designed us to have
children is not good enough, and we can do better."

	In fact, he says, the biggest battle in the next century probably
will be over natural childbirth:  Should it remain legal?

	"Why should anyone let a woman and a man have a baby the old-
fashioned way," he asks, "when they could have a technologically mediated,
genetically screened, environmentally optimal birth by using in-vitro
fertilization, embryo biopsy (genetic testing) and an artificial uterus,
the neonatal unit of tomorrow?"

	Too far out?  Maybe.  "A single cell is such a complicated thing.
The idea that we will know everything about it in 50 years is just
incredibly arrogant," says geneticist Magdalena Plewinska of the University
of Miami.

	Yet the boutique baby and other mind-bending scenarios are "exactly
what you would expect in a culture that has gone mad," says Stanley
Hauerwas, a theology professor at the Duke University Divinity School.
"The attempt in the name of compassion to create a world without limits
fails to see that the limits are what make us human and humane.

	"Take the question of whether you should try to eliminate retardation.
People assume the answer to that is yes, like we want to eliminate cancer.
But the difference is, you can't eliminate retardation without eliminating
the retarded."

	And eliminating the retarded would diminish our lives, Hauerwas says.
"The mentally handicapped are simply there to beckon us into a world that
otherwise we would not imagine.  It is good to be around people you don't
choose.  They teach you how to live."

	[There ought to be a law...  I vote this guy Most Entropic Being
of the Year.  -MV]

	Many theologians and ethicists complain that society and the media
have been too naive about new technology.  Unpoliced use of genetic
knowledge can be dangerous, despite good intentions, they warn.

	Theologian Richard John Neuhaus warns of the "unmistakable
similarities" between Nazi Germany's use of genetics and the direction
in which research is moving.  The goal of both, he writes, is to
"produce healthier human beings, and, perhaps, a better quality of
human being."

	To guard against such dangers [?!? -MV], 3 percent of the Genome
Project's $3 billion budget has been set aside for grants to explore the
ethical, legal, and social implications of new technology.  "The best
prohibition against the misuses of this information are the definition
and strengthening of the moral values in our society," says Nancy Wexler,
ethics chairman of the project.

	Some ethicists say society is not up to the moral task at hand.

	"People need to understand what's entailed in genetics, in gene
therapy and screening, but they also need to be able to analyze and
criticize it from a moral and religious framework," says Therese Lyslaught,
a theological ethicist at Parkridge Center for the Study of Health, Faith
and Ethics in Chicago.  "It seems to me neither is going on, but science
is moving forward.  The danger is that we'll start legislating things
that will affect our lives without really knowing what they mean."

	Fifty-eight percent of 1,000 people queried in a March of Dimes
