Date: Mon, 15 Mar 93 21:12:28 GMT
From: sjw@liberty.demon.co.uk (Stephen J. Whitrow)
Subject: reason

Nick Szabo mentions the idea of assigning values to beliefs; I posted some
thoughts on this last October. There are three important factors I take into
consideration, and the ideal world-view would perform well in each of these
measures. 

1) It must not conflict with the empirical evidence, and elements of the
hypothesis (or grand unified theory) must be consistent with each other.

2) It should motivate the individual to carry out strategies and behave in
ways which are to his / her personal self-interest (the collective benefit of
the species is also taken into account but in any case should be roughly
aligned with self-interest -- especially for long-lived individuals).

3) It should optimise the subjective mental states and self-esteem of the
individual.

It's often a compromise between 2) and 3). Frequently some non-falsifiable
'facts' are included; personally I think this is o.k. provided they do not
contravene the limits of natural law. I'd say incorporating the existence of
'supernatural' phenomena or being(s) would fail to satisfy 1), as on the one
hand you have natural laws that have been scientifically derived, and yet they
are allegedly being violated. Others may not worry if the non-falsifiable
'facts' are supernaturalistic.....

The 'hyper-rational', or perhaps I mean 'arch-empirical', approach of denying
the validity of _any_ non-falsifiable hypothesis and always using Occam's
Razor, doesn't always seem to take 3) into account, whilst the supernaturalist
/ mystical epistemology often fails dismally on 2) with tragic entropic
consequences. Although I would note that rationalism often satisfies all three
requirements rather well -- consider how many people (Perry for instance) have
remarked that they felt much better in themselves after rejecting the
conventional altruist's mode of thought.

Since individuals are not the same, I don't believe that the optimum
world-view for one individual is necessarily the best for another -- in the
medium term it would seem reasonable for individuals to select a world-view 
that is tailor-made for them personally. There is room for philosophic freedom
now; maybe in the long run our views will have to converge and we will then be
able to claim that our 20/21st century beliefs helped us to get through the
archaic period which included far too much statism, deathism, suffering,
murder, poverty, warfare, insanity, misery, hatred, foolishness, crime,
genocide etc. All the undesirable garbage from our biological heritage that
we've had to carry along.  

>From my point of view, I would claim that if a person's belief system offers
them some prospects of progressing to becoming a superbeing with a brain the
size of Jupiter (or even dozens of brains the size of England dispersed 
throughout the galaxies), that belief system is far more valuable to them than
one that guarantees that in forty years or so they'll be roasted in the oven
of a crematorium or buried in a coffin. I know those that I might consider
'irrationalists' or 'deathists' are quite entitled to think of my view as
being totally wrong; they cannot be expected to share the same set of values.
And in fact allowing individuals the freedom to choose their own beliefs
according to their own values is a positive-sum game. If memetic collectivism
were somehow imposed, it would be a sorry day for almost everyone.

Steve Whitrow                       sjw@liberty.demon.co.uk

