I retract the claim of intellectual dishonesty; this was a mistake made on
my part in reacting to the inflammatory part without fully thinking through
a reasonably response.  I also forgot that you weren't yet here during the
intellectual property wars where some of this was hashed out.

I still argue that it is inflammatory in that you have overgeneralized and
implied a negative connotation with no real data to support it.  However,
it did get my attention!  

>It is not intellectually dishonest, because I really don't understand
>what the *fundamental* difference between owning a piece of land and
>owning a configuration of bits is.

This is a much better way to state the problem as it brings it down to
fundamentals without being inflammatory.  I think in making your
inflammatory statement you do give an impression of intellectual laziness
in that I think there are certain deductions you could have made based on
information that has appeared on this list that would at least partially
negate your statement.

1. Given a system of PPL enclaves, I don't think most anarcho-libertarians
would move to prevent the development of a PPL that provided protection for
information we currently label as intellectual property.  In particular, if
such a PPL did arise and it did significantly stimulate the production of
useful information, then it would be likely to outcompete it's competitors.

2. This is more of a stretch, but I think there has been some evidence that
some entities on this list distinguish between those sorts of
information that can be copyrighted and those that can be patented and
support the former without supporting the latter.  Thus some libertarians
are opposed to some but not all forms of intellectual property.

At this point, after hopefully backing and filling myself out of the hole I
dug, I'd like to make what I hope is a constructive solution to this list.

Perhaps those involved in the past intellectual property thread could
repost some of the more succint posts to give Dale a flavor of some of the
arguments. 

	Scott


