buncombe.  In my original post I chose alchemy as an analogy for a
reason, viz. that even those alchemists (another example
would be physicians/herbalists a la Paracelsus) who did achieve
practical results, nevertheless tried to explain those results
in theoretical terms that were absolute nonsense.  That's probably
what you've got here, though nothing that you quoted shows that
this author even has much in the way of practical results.

Derek:
> And it gets worse.  The whole point of the exercises is actually
> to put forward a mystical MORAL dogma -- whether it be the
> interconnectedness of life, reverence for a world-spirit, or
> whatever, I can't see why phenomenal investigations carefully
> designed to support a particular (religious) set of values should
> necessarily be optimal or even useful for those of us inclined
> toward more rational transhumanist eupraxophies.

Neither can I.  But, "carefully designed to support a particular
(religious) set of values" -- what are we talking about here?  Is
this still MacDonald?  I'd have a problem with this as a characterization
of the entire universe of practices that I'd want to discuss in this
connection.

(me):
> > pulling your consciousness away from the word-stream and
> > the stream of visual and auditory percepts that are your
> > normal phenomenological environment.  OK, given a model
> > of consciousness--Dennett's, say--can you make it make
> > any predictions about what the effect of
> > such an exercise might be?

Derek replies with some suggestions along the lines of how this
might make it easier to focus your attention, etc.  I found these kind 
of unimaginative; let me try:

Dennet, like the psychologist Roger Brown, suggests that
the "self" that we feel continually aware of is not a stored pattern,
organizing and directing the proceedings of consciousness--which is
how we experience it phenomenologically--but an epiphenomenon of 
a continuous stream of "self"-defining narrative.  Well, I can't
read this without being reminded of the Buddhist teaching that the
Self is an illusion, and that by various meditative and other
practices it is possible to see through that illusion.  Just stated
baldly like that this seems like nothing but an article of faith,
and a rather improbable one at that.  But once Dennet and Brown
start advocating similar-sounded ideas (with no esoteric or mystical
influence in either case, as far as I can tell) I begin to wonder
whether the Buddhist claim might actually represent a summary of
potentially reproducible results of experiments in consciousness.
After all, "illusion" is a reasonable pre-Dennettian interpretation
of his phenomenological reality.  Now, if Dennett and Brown are right
that maintaining a stream of narrative is a major source of this "illusion",
then the mystics might well be right that a sufficiently radical
interruption of that stream can provide a glimpse of what some of
the mind looks like without the phenomenological "self" at center
stage.  If they are, and you follow their prescriptions, then would
what you see be the phenomenological correlate of some other aspect
of Dennett's (or someone else's) model, or perhaps input into a
new model?  After all, what other sort of direct (dis)confirmation of
models like Dennet's could there be?

Scott DeLancey







